 {"n": 5, "week": 2, "title": "Understanding RAG (retrieval-augmented generation)",
  "understand": "RAG is an open-book exam. Before the model answers, your app looks up the right pages in your documents and hands them to the model with the question.",
  "why": "A model only knows what it was trained on. It does not know your leave policy, last week's report or a customer's contract. RAG gives it the right pages at answer time, which is cheaper and easier to update than fine-tuning.",
  "how": ["Document -> ingestion -> extraction -> chunking -> embedding -> vector database -> retrieval -> LLM -> answer with citations.", "Use RAG when knowledge changes often or must be cited. Do not use it to teach a new style or skill; that is fine-tuning.", "Compare with plain search: search returns documents; RAG returns a written answer grounded in them."],
  "figure": "rag", "example": "04_rag_toy.py",
  "explain": ["chunk() splits the document into overlapping pieces, so an answer that spans a boundary is not lost.", "embed() here counts words. A real system uses an embedding model that understands meaning.", "cosine() measures how similar two vectors are: 1 means the same direction, 0 means unrelated.", "retrieve() ranks all chunks and returns the top k."],
  "test": "Ask a question whose answer is in the document; the top chunk should contain it.",
  "troubleshoot": ["Right answer not retrieved: chunks may be too small or too large; try different sizes and overlap.", "Answer invents details: the model was not given the chunks, or the prompt did not say 'answer only from the context'."],
  "pm": ["RAG quality depends on the document pipeline as much as the model. Budget time for document cleaning, and measure retrieval separately from answer quality."],
  "assignment": "Change chunk size to 8 and to 20 words. For three questions, record which chunk ranks first each time, and write one sentence on what changed.",
  "interview": [("When would you choose RAG instead of fine-tuning?", "When knowledge changes often, must be cited, or is large. Fine-tuning changes behaviour and style better than it stores facts."), ("Why do chunks overlap?", "So information that sits across a chunk boundary is still fully contained in at least one chunk.")],
  "links": [("AIPM2 14: RAG (PDF)", "handbook/aipm2/AIPM2-14-Retrieval-Augmented-Generation-RAG.pdf"), ("Customising models: prompting vs RAG vs fine-tuning", "data-science/lessons/genai-10-customising-models-prompting-vs-rag-vs-fine-tuning-lora-dist.html")]},

